task(TASK-STATUS-VOCABULARY-NORMALISE):
  title    = "close the status vocabulary in TASKS.vsm — 25 values, 108 tasks unreadable"
  status   = done
  priority = alta
  date     = "2026-08-10"
  owner    = "[S] DS executes — [O] measured and specified, rmw3 approved 2026-08-10"
  Verified-By: task-maintenance-valid
  resolved_date: "2026-08-22"
  context:
    "Measured 2026-08-10 during the boot audit, on the file as it stands after the archive pass:
       25 distinct values in the `status` field across 360 task blocks
       108 tasks whose status NO parser can read (143 KB of the file)
     Consequences already observed, not hypothetical:
       - onboard.sh section 0 could not state its own completeness. It reported 11 open tasks for
         months because it parsed one of two task SYNTAXES; that is fixed, but it still cannot
         vouch for the 108 whose STATUS is unreadable.
       - state-canon's tasks_provider inherits the same blindness.
       - task(BRANCH-RECONCILE) sat `open` for 5 weeks describing a divergence that no longer
         existed. A closed vocabulary does not prevent that, but an open one makes it undetectable.
       - the archive pass (scripts/state/tasks_archive.py --closed) can only move `done`/`closed`
         bodies safely. TASKS.vsm is 691 KB and the 256 KB read limit is NOT reachable until the
         remaining statuses are decidable. This task is what unblocks the second pass."
  design:
    "Dispatched to DS: docs/deploy/2026-08-10_ds_dispatch_task_status_vocabulary.vsm.
     (1) Enumerate every distinct status with its count and an example task.
     (2) Propose a CLOSED vocabulary and a mapping old -> new. Do not invent semantics: where a
         status is genuinely ambiguous (`parked`, `phase_abc_done_d_pending`,
         `build_test_done_awaiting_s5_golive_decision`), list it for rmw3 rather than guessing.
     (3) Apply the mapping mechanically; declare the vocabulary where the parser can enforce it.
     (4) [O] then re-runs tasks_archive.py --closed to finish the size reduction."
  gating   = "none — DS can start on dispatch. Step (2)'s ambiguous set needs rmw3 before step (3)."
  files    = [TASKS.vsm, scripts/state/tasks_archive.py, scripts/tasks_validate.py]
  found_by = "boot audit 2026-08-10, item 4 / option C"

task(NUCLEUS-CHAIN-RESTART-SEAM):
  title    = "nucleus chain: prev_hash=null on every restart (read_TASKS.vsm_hash fragility)"
  status   = done
    // EOF TASKS.vsm-read (64KB, utf8-lossy) + LOUD failure + self-documenting chain_seam
    // event (feeds TRIAGE-EVIDENCE seam closure). Rust, build 108.
  priority = low
  date     = "2026-07-02"
  context:
    "Observed at the 03:18Z restart: first post-restart event carries prev_hash=null —
     read_TASKS.vsm_hash() returned None so the chain starts a fresh segment each restart
     instead of linking to the disk TASKS.vsm. Each restart therefore produces one
     legitimate 'restart seam' merkle finding in s3-star's window (the pattern the
     auto-triage note was originally written for). read_TASKS.vsm_hash reads the ENTIRE
     disk events.jsonl (~370k lines) via fs::read_to_string — one invalid UTF-8 byte
     or memory pressure makes it silently return None. Fix idea: read only the last
     line (seek from EOF), and log loudly when TASKS.vsm resume fails instead of silently
     starting a new segment. Rust change → certify on 108 per R1/R2."
  file     = projects/vsf-nucleus/src/telemetry_canonical.rs

