      got synced back into ~/vsf/LICENSE. Fixed same session (see commit
      cc5ac5d and the follow-up docs-clause/watermark commit) -- this task
      is about a SEPARATE, more serious drift found while verifying the
      license's cited Covenant SHA-256.",
  covenant_drift_confirmed: "sha256sum across all 4 known copies of
      covenant.vsm: ~/vsf/docs/governance/covenant.vsm and
      n01:~/vsf-bastion/covenant.vsm BOTH hash dde12ead... (matches what
      the LICENSE cites -- these two are correct and in sync). But
      n01:~/vsf-api/covenant.vsm hashes d09bf6c7... and
      n01:~/vsf-sandbox/covenant.vsm hashes d6a44230... -- THREE different
      covenant.vsm contents across 4 locations, not two.",
  why_this_matters_more_than_the_license_drift: "The covenant is the
      document the kernel is SUPPOSED to cryptographically verify at
      startup (LICENSE CONDITIONS: 'The kernel verifies the Covenant Hash
      at startup... may not circumvent, patch, or disable'). If different
      deployments (vsf-api, vsf-sandbox) are actually running against
      genuinely different covenant content, 'covenant integrity
      verification' as a concept breaks down -- there is no longer one
      canonical document being verified, only a hash-matches-itself
      tautology at each site independently.",
  not_investigated_yet: "Did NOT diff the actual content differences
      between the 3 versions (could be trivial -- e.g. a stale copy from
      before a real edit -- or could be substantive, e.g. different
      A0/A0.1 wording or different signatories). Did NOT check which
      version each site's KERNEL actually verifies against at runtime
      (vsf-api and vsf-sandbox might not even run kernel code that does
      startup verification -- worth confirming before assuming this is
      live-exploitable, not just a stale-file problem).",
  next: "Diff the 3 divergent covenant.vsm versions to characterize the
      actual differences (cosmetic vs substantive). Determine which
